2026-06-30 15:44:30,107 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-aed89919-c06e-4f8d-ade6-49958a35c0a0', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35651_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample1.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35653_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample2.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35655_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample3.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35657_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample4.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35659_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample5.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35661_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample6.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35663_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample7.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35665_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample8.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35686_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample9.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35688_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample10.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35690_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample11.raw\nPSB-905DDA_UPRC_3702_E35692_2p_uPAC27_PM7_sample12.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nA Nitrogen-specific Interactome Analysis Sheds Light on the Role of the SnRK1 and TOR Kinases in\nPlant Nitrogen Signaling - part2 kinase assay\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nNitrogen (N) is of utmost importance for plant growth and development. Multiple studies have shown that N signaling is tightly coupled with carbon (C) levels, but the interplay between C/N metabolism and growth remains largely an enigma. Nonetheless, the protein kinases sucrose non-fermenting 1 (SNF1)-related kinase 1 (SnRK1) and target of rapamycin (TOR), two ancient central metabolic regulators, are emerging as key integrators that link C/N status with growth. Despite their pivotal importance, the exact mechanisms behind the sensing of N status and its integration with C availability to drive metabolic decisions are largely unknown. Especially for SnRK1 it is not clear how this kinase responds to altered N levels. Therefore, we first monitored N-dependent SnRK1 kinase activity at high resolution with our in vivo separation of phase-based activity reporter of kinase (SPARK), revealing a contrasting N-dependency in shoot and root tissues. Next, using affinity purification (AP) and proximity labeling (PL) coupled to mass spectrometry (MS) experiments, we constructed an N-specific SnRK1 and TOR interactome in Arabidopsis thaliana cell cultures during N-starved and\xa0 repleted growth conditions. To broaden our understanding of the N-dependency of the TOR/SnRK1 signaling pathway, the resulting network was compared to corresponding C-related networks, identifying a large number of novel, N-specific interactors. Moreover, through integration of N dependent transcriptome and phosphoproteome data, we were able to pinpoint additional N dependent network components, revealing SnRK1 regulatory proteins that might function at the crosstalk of C/N signaling. Finally, confirmation of known and identification of novel interactors, such as IRE1A and RAB18, indicate that the endoplasmic reticulum functions as a key regulatory hub for TOR and SnRK1 via integration of C/N signaling. Part2, kinase assay on RAB18, PANK2, SEC12 and NLP8.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor kinase assays coupled to mass spectrometry, recombinant His-MBP fusion proteins were incubated with His-MBP-KIN10 or His-MBP-KIN10-K48M (kinase-dead). Kinase reactions were performed overnight 4h at 30°C, combining 2.5\u2009μL kinase with 5\u2009μL substrate in kinase assay buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl (pH 8.0), 1 mM EGTA, 2.5 mM DTT, 5 mM MgCl2, 50 uM cold ATP, 1x PhosSTOP). Reactions were stopped by addition of NuPAGE LDS sample buffer (NP0007, Invitrogen) and NuPAGE Sample reducing agent (MP0004, Invitrogen) and incubated for 10\u2009min at 70°C. Proteins were separated by SDS-PAGE on a NuPAGE gel and stained with Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250. Gel excision and preparation of the samples for MS was further completed as described in Van Leene et al., 2015. LC-MS/MS analysis Peptides were re-dissolved in 20 μl loading solvent A (0.1% TFA in water/ACN (98:2, v/v)), of which 4 μl was injected for LC-MS/MS analysis on an Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano LC (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Bremen, Germany) in-line connected to a Q Exactive mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The peptides were first loaded on a trapping column that was made in-house, 100 μm internal diameter (I.D.) × 20 mm, 5 μm beads C18 Reprosil-HD (Dr. Maisch, Ammerbuch-Entringen, Germany). After flushing from the trapping column, peptides were separated on a 50 cm μPAC™ column with C18-endcapped functionality (Pharmafluidics, Belgium) kept at a constant temperature of 50°C. Peptides were eluted by a nonlinear gradient from 99% solvent A’ (0.1% formic acid in water) reaching 10% solvent B’ (0.1% formic acid in water/ACN, 20/80 (v/v))  in 9 min, 33% solvent B’ in 32 min, 55% solvent B’ in 43 min, 70% solvent B’ in 48 min, at a flow rate of 300 nL/min, followed by a 2 min wash with 70% solvent B’. The mass spectrometer was operated in data-dependent, positive ionization mode, automatically switching between MS and MS/MS acquisition for the 5 most abundant peaks in each MS spectrum. Full-scan MS spectra (400–2,000 m/z) were acquired at a resolution of 70,000 in the Orbitrap analyzer after accumulation to a target value of 3,000,000. The 5 most intense ions above a threshold value of 13,000 were isolated with a width of 2 m/z for fragmentation at a normalized collision energy of 25% after filling the trap at a target value of 50,000 for maximum 80 ms. MS/MS spectra (200–2000 m/z) were acquired at a resolution of 17,500 in the Orbitrap analyzer.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nArabidopsis thaliana (mouse-ear cress)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
