2026-06-30 15:44:36,805 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d88f2448-fdfd-406d-9636-6885ebe2934e', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nA Nitrogen-specific Interactome Analysis Sheds Light on the Role of the SnRK1 and TOR Kinases in\nPlant Nitrogen Signaling - part2 kinase assay\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nNitrogen (N) is of utmost importance for plant growth and development. Multiple studies have shown that N signaling is tightly coupled with carbon (C) levels, but the interplay between C/N metabolism and growth remains largely an enigma. Nonetheless, the protein kinases sucrose non-fermenting 1 (SNF1)-related kinase 1 (SnRK1) and target of rapamycin (TOR), two ancient central metabolic regulators, are emerging as key integrators that link C/N status with growth. Despite their pivotal importance, the exact mechanisms behind the sensing of N status and its integration with C availability to drive metabolic decisions are largely unknown. Especially for SnRK1 it is not clear how this kinase responds to altered N levels. Therefore, we first monitored N-dependent SnRK1 kinase activity at high resolution with our in vivo separation of phase-based activity reporter of kinase (SPARK), revealing a contrasting N-dependency in shoot and root tissues. Next, using affinity purification (AP) and proximity labeling (PL) coupled to mass spectrometry (MS) experiments, we constructed an N-specific SnRK1 and TOR interactome in Arabidopsis thaliana cell cultures during N-starved and\xa0 repleted growth conditions. To broaden our understanding of the N-dependency of the TOR/SnRK1 signaling pathway, the resulting network was compared to corresponding C-related networks, identifying a large number of novel, N-specific interactors. Moreover, through integration of N dependent transcriptome and phosphoproteome data, we were able to pinpoint additional N dependent network components, revealing SnRK1 regulatory proteins that might function at the crosstalk of C/N signaling. Finally, confirmation of known and identification of novel interactors, such as IRE1A and RAB18, indicate that the endoplasmic reticulum functions as a key regulatory hub for TOR and SnRK1 via integration of C/N signaling. Part2, kinase assay on RAB18, PANK2, SEC12 and NLP8.\n\n[METHODS]\nFor kinase assays coupled to mass spectrometry, recombinant His-MBP fusion proteins were incubated with His-MBP-KIN10 or His-MBP-KIN10-K48M (kinase-dead). Kinase reactions were performed overnight 4h at 30°C, combining 2.5\u2009μL kinase with 5\u2009μL substrate in kinase assay buffer (50 mM Tris-HCl (pH 8.0), 1 mM EGTA, 2.5 mM DTT, 5 mM MgCl2, 50 uM cold ATP, 1x PhosSTOP). Reactions were stopped by addition of NuPAGE LDS sample buffer (NP0007, Invitrogen) and NuPAGE Sample reducing agent (MP0004, Invitrogen) and incubated for 10\u2009min at 70°C. Proteins were separated by SDS-PAGE on a NuPAGE gel and stained with Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250. Gel excision and preparation of the samples for MS was further completed as described in Van Leene et al., 2015. LC-MS/MS analysis Peptides were re-dissolved in 20 μl loading solvent A (0.1% TFA in water/ACN (98:2, v/v)), of which 4 μl was injected for LC-MS/MS analysis on an Ultimate 3000 RSLC nano LC (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Bremen, Germany) in-line connected to a Q Exactive mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific). The peptides were first loaded on a trapping column that was made in-house, 100 μm internal diameter (I.D.) × 20 mm, 5 μm beads C18 Reprosil-HD (Dr. Maisch, Ammerbuch-Entringen, Germany). After flushing from the trapping column, peptides were separated on a 50 cm μPAC™ column with C18-endcapped functionality (Pharmafluidics, Belgium) kept at a constant temperature of 50°C. Peptides were eluted by a nonlinear gradient from 99% solvent A’ (0.1% formic acid in water) reaching 10% solvent B’ (0.1% formic acid in water/ACN, 20/80 (v/v))  in 9 min, 33% solvent B’ in 32 min, 55% solvent B’ in 43 min, 70% solvent B’ in 48 min, at a flow rate of 300 nL/min, followed by a 2 min wash with 70% solvent B’. The mass spectrometer was operated in data-dependent, positive ionization mode, automatically switching between MS and MS/MS acquisition for the 5 most abundant peaks in each MS spectrum. Full-scan MS spectra (400–2,000 m/z) were acquired at a resolution of 70,000 in the Orbitrap analyzer after accumulation to a target value of 3,000,000. The 5 most intense ions above a threshold value of 13,000 were isolated with a width of 2 m/z for fragmentation at a normalized collision energy of 25% after filling the trap at a target value of 50,000 for maximum 80 ms. MS/MS spectra (200–2000 m/z) were acquired at a resolution of 17,500 in the Orbitrap analyzer.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
